    "q-81336": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81336",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14272737/assets/c497efbb28bade962b997d7e14f5a272f0cead56f7567b1baaebae695c7c80f4.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=e,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $y={e}^{x},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & =e \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14272737/assets/95a6d9b0bc464c719311dc900967ebd06a3aa3d3004cfe5303359eecb20551e7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{e}^{x}\\le y\\le e\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
